WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:01.614
Welcome back!

00:00:01.614 --> 00:00:04.536
In this Java lesson, you'll choose types, or

00:00:04.536 --> 00:00:07.592
kinds of values, for variables, the names that

00:00:07.592 --> 00:00:09.252
hold your program's data.

00:00:09.252 --> 00:00:11.710
You'll calculate a total and trace an

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:14.765
assignment, which replaces a variable's stored

00:00:14.765 --> 00:00:15.164
value.

00:00:15.808 --> 00:00:19.247
These skills let software keep track of changing

00:00:19.247 --> 00:00:22.256
information, from available seats to order

00:00:22.256 --> 00:00:25.552
totals, while choosing the right kind of value

00:00:25.552 --> 00:00:26.483
for each job.

00:00:27.139 --> 00:00:30.091
A campus club needs a small ticket record.

00:00:30.091 --> 00:00:33.465
We'll store a whole-number ticket count, a price

00:00:33.465 --> 00:00:36.628
in dollars for each ticket, and whether sales

00:00:36.628 --> 00:00:37.261
are open.

00:00:37.261 --> 00:00:40.564
The program will calculate a total, then record

00:00:40.564 --> 00:00:41.689
a changed count.

00:00:42.332 --> 00:00:45.616
We want to see the calculated total, the updated

00:00:45.616 --> 00:00:48.489
ticket count, and the sales status in that

00:00:48.489 --> 00:00:48.900
order.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:52.047
The values will be supplied in the program, so

00:00:52.047 --> 00:00:55.057
no one needs to type an order while it runs.

00:00:55.099 --> 00:00:58.290
Let's open Main.java in the Workspace and build

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:01.142
a practice record before we try the club's

00:01:01.142 --> 00:01:01.549
order.

00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:08.564
We'll use the starter structure from the last

00:01:08.564 --> 00:01:09.068
lesson.

00:01:09.068 --> 00:01:12.385
Main is the class that holds our code, and its

00:01:12.385 --> 00:01:15.630
main method is the named operation where this

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:16.711
program starts.

00:01:16.711 --> 00:01:19.739
Our ticket instructions belong inside that

00:01:19.739 --> 00:01:20.244
method.

00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:28.540
With a place for our instructions, we can name

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.645
the information the club needs.

00:01:30.645 --> 00:01:32.953
The practice count is two tickets.

00:01:32.953 --> 00:01:36.143
We'll use int because a ticket count is a whole

00:01:36.143 --> 00:01:36.618
number.

00:01:38.973 --> 00:01:42.071
This declaration introduces tickets with its

00:01:42.071 --> 00:01:43.056
type and name.

00:01:43.056 --> 00:01:45.591
Giving it two as a starting value is

00:01:45.591 --> 00:01:46.647
initialization.

00:01:46.647 --> 00:01:49.744
The next value is a price, which can include

00:01:49.744 --> 00:01:53.053
part of a dollar, so we'll give ticketPrice the

00:01:53.053 --> 00:01:53.898
double type.

00:01:56.968 --> 00:01:59.980
The practice price is five dollars per ticket.

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:02.992
A double can hold fractional values, though it

00:02:02.992 --> 00:02:04.760
approximates some decimals.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.707
The prices in this example can be represented

00:02:07.707 --> 00:02:08.231
exactly.

00:02:08.231 --> 00:02:10.784
Sales status is different: it answers a

00:02:10.784 --> 00:02:12.028
yes-or-no question.

00:02:12.028 --> 00:02:14.975
We'll use boolean, which holds true or false,

00:02:14.975 --> 00:02:17.660
and start this practice record with sales

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:18.118
closed.

00:02:21.134 --> 00:02:24.007
We now have a count, a price, and a sales

00:02:24.007 --> 00:02:27.020
status, each with a suitable built-in type.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:30.314
To find the cost of the tickets, we'll multiply

00:02:30.314 --> 00:02:33.538
the count by the price and store the result in

00:02:33.538 --> 00:02:33.958
total.

00:02:33.958 --> 00:02:37.322
That calculation is an arithmetic expression: it

00:02:37.322 --> 00:02:39.284
produces a value we can use.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:46.028
For this practice record, two tickets at five

00:02:46.028 --> 00:02:48.093
dollars each make ten dollars.

00:02:48.093 --> 00:02:51.123
Total stores the result of that calculation.

00:02:51.123 --> 00:02:54.014
It isn't a live formula linked to tickets.

00:02:54.014 --> 00:02:57.250
To check that, let's replace the count with six

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:00.210
before displaying total, the count, and the

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:01.105
sales status.

00:03:11.102 --> 00:03:14.378
This assignment reuses tickets without declaring

00:03:14.378 --> 00:03:14.992
it again.

00:03:14.992 --> 00:03:17.858
We'll print total after the update, so the

00:03:17.858 --> 00:03:21.134
output can show whether its stored value changed

00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:21.407
too.

00:03:21.407 --> 00:03:24.546
Let's close the starter structure to check the

00:03:24.546 --> 00:03:26.252
complete practice record.

00:03:27.571 --> 00:03:29.915
The practice program is complete.

00:03:29.915 --> 00:03:33.111
Let's click Run and check whether total still

00:03:33.111 --> 00:03:35.953
holds ten dollars after the ticket count

00:03:35.953 --> 00:03:36.521
changes.

00:03:41.716 --> 00:03:43.840
The first line is ten point zero.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.930
We printed total after changing tickets, so this

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:49.698
shows that total kept the ten-dollar result

00:03:49.698 --> 00:03:50.921
calculated earlier.

00:03:50.921 --> 00:03:53.625
The second line is six, the updated count.

00:03:53.625 --> 00:03:56.457
The last line is false because we left sales

00:03:56.457 --> 00:03:56.908
closed.

00:03:56.908 --> 00:03:59.997
Assignment changed tickets without repeating the

00:03:59.997 --> 00:04:02.765
multiplication or changing the other stored

00:04:02.765 --> 00:04:03.216
values.

00:04:03.315 --> 00:04:06.345
That practice run gives us a pattern for the

00:04:06.345 --> 00:04:07.792
club's actual record.

00:04:07.792 --> 00:04:11.097
We'll replace the instructions inside main while

00:04:11.097 --> 00:04:13.439
keeping the same class and method.

00:04:13.439 --> 00:04:16.745
This time the starting count is three, the price

00:04:16.745 --> 00:04:19.982
is four dollars and fifty cents per ticket, and

00:04:19.982 --> 00:04:21.015
sales are open.

00:04:28.922 --> 00:04:31.726
The club's starting values are in place.

00:04:31.726 --> 00:04:34.952
We'll use the same multiplication to store the

00:04:34.952 --> 00:04:35.793
order total.

00:04:35.793 --> 00:04:38.668
This time, unlike the practice run, we'll

00:04:38.668 --> 00:04:41.472
display total before changing the count.

00:04:49.534 --> 00:04:52.800
The total calculation comes before the update.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:55.924
Let's finish the record by assigning four to

00:04:55.924 --> 00:04:59.047
tickets, then displaying the count and sales

00:04:59.047 --> 00:05:02.384
status, just as we did in the practice program.

00:05:10.348 --> 00:05:12.233
The club's program is ready.

00:05:12.233 --> 00:05:15.331
Before we run it, write the three output lines

00:05:15.331 --> 00:05:15.937
in order.

00:05:15.937 --> 00:05:18.900
For each print statement, ask which value is

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:20.314
stored at that point.

00:05:20.314 --> 00:05:23.546
Use the practice run to decide whether the later

00:05:23.546 --> 00:05:26.441
assignment changes the earlier calculation.

00:05:26.441 --> 00:05:28.798
Pause here if you'd like more time.

00:05:36.866 --> 00:05:40.071
Let's click Run to check your prediction against

00:05:40.071 --> 00:05:41.741
the club's actual record.

00:05:46.862 --> 00:05:50.077
The total is thirteen point five dollars: three

00:05:50.077 --> 00:05:53.086
tickets multiplied by four dollars and fifty

00:05:53.086 --> 00:05:53.497
cents.

00:05:53.497 --> 00:05:56.712
Four is the updated count, and true means sales

00:05:56.712 --> 00:05:57.327
are open.

00:05:57.327 --> 00:06:00.542
Compare those three lines with your prediction.

00:06:00.542 --> 00:06:03.757
The update replaces tickets after the total was

00:06:03.757 --> 00:06:06.561
calculated; it doesn't change the earlier

00:06:06.561 --> 00:06:07.587
printed result.

00:06:07.736 --> 00:06:10.068
We've built the club's ticket record.

00:06:10.068 --> 00:06:12.841
Int kept the count as a whole number, double

00:06:12.841 --> 00:06:15.174
held the price and total, and boolean

00:06:15.174 --> 00:06:17.443
represented whether sales were open.

00:06:17.443 --> 00:06:20.468
The expression calculated a cost, and assignment

00:06:20.468 --> 00:06:22.044
changed one stored value.

00:06:22.044 --> 00:06:25.069
Tracing the statements in order explains why the

00:06:25.069 --> 00:06:27.780
output contains both an earlier total and a

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:28.536
later count.

00:06:29.263 --> 00:06:31.991
For your next experiment, change only the

00:06:31.991 --> 00:06:33.988
starting ticket count to five.

00:06:33.988 --> 00:06:37.182
What total should appear, and which other output

00:06:37.182 --> 00:06:38.978
lines should stay the same?

00:06:38.978 --> 00:06:41.706
Explain your reasoning before you run the

00:06:41.706 --> 00:06:42.638
program again.
